// EXPECT-DIAG: E134
// EXPECT-DIAG: Cannot explicitly cast 'int[]' to 'ptr'
// FDN-002 (docs/planning/ROADMAP_v2.0.0.md §27 D23, docs/development/changes/
// FDN-002.md): D23's own wording is "an unsized T[] parameter converted to
// ptr and returned must be rejected". EscapePass DOES carry that rule
// (classifyProvenance()'s CastExprAST case, src/Analysis.cpp) for the day
// it becomes reachable, but verified live while writing this test: `arr to
// ptr` for an array-typed `arr` is ALREADY rejected today, one pass
// earlier, by the pre-existing array/pointer type-compatibility rule
// (Types.cpp's typesCompatible(), "one side is an array, the other isn't"
// -- now E134 "Cannot explicitly cast 'int[]' to 'ptr'") -- there is currently
// NO legal way to produce a `ptr` from a `T[]` at all, by cast or by
// implicit coercion (only `string` has an extern-call ptr coercion,
// isExternStringToPtrCoercion(), TypeCheckerCall.cpp; arrays have none).
// So this file still correctly fails to compile -- D23's hazard cannot
// occur -- but via the PRE-EXISTING type-mismatch diagnostic, not
// EscapePass's E086; see FDN-002.md's "Unresolved questions" for the full
// account. `unsafe module;` is needed only so `ptr` is legal in this
// ordinary function's signature at all (checkNoRawPointersInSignature,
// TypeCheckerCore.cpp) -- unrelated to what this test actually exercises.
unsafe module;

fn arrayAsPtr(arr: int[]): ptr {
    return arr to ptr;
}

fn main(): int {
    mut a: int[5] = [1, 2, 3, 4, 5];
    unsafe {
        mut p: ptr = arrayAsPtr(a);
    }
    return 0;
}
